to an artifact.\n\nTHE SECOND QUESTION IS A CONTRADICTION INSIDE THE CONDITIONS, and I think it is a genuine one rather than codex being strict. Condition 4 says nothing about the resident, MCP or HTTP surfaces changes behaviour. Condition 2 says the CLI must reach the existing selectors through one shared code path rather than a second implementation. Adding state and reaches to ArtifactQuery satisfies condition 2 exactly — it is the shared type the resident HTTP handler decodes — and by doing so it necessarily widens the HTTP query contract, which is what condition 4 forbids. The two cannot both hold for any implementation that adds a selector at all.\n\nCodex's remedy is to align the surfaces and documentation or change the condition. Aligning means exposing state and reaches through MCP too, which today exposes neither and requires paths, and documenting the widened HTTP contract. That is more surface than your request asked for and I would rather not decide it alone. My reading is that condition 4 meant no behaviour change for existing callers — which holds, since state defaults to live and is byte-identical to the old behaviour, and an omitted reaches selects nothing new — rather than no new optional field ever. If that is what you meant, say so and I will document the widened contract without touching MCP. If you meant the stricter thing, the alignment work is a child request and I will file it.\n\nWHAT I AM DOING MEANWHILE, so nothing waits on you unnecessarily: fixing the two findings that are unambiguously mine, on a new head. The scope and contract answers change what else goes into it, so I would rather have them before I request the next review than after."}